=== day
n: 1
title: Your first web page
subtitle: HTML
hours: 3 hours
page: show.html
slot: article | html | show.html · inside <article>
refs: @page

--- intro
Today you build a web page about one TV show, **Friends**, using **HTML**: the language that tells the browser what each piece of a page *is*.

You start with an empty box and add one thing at a time: a heading, a paragraph, a picture, lists, a link and a table. The top bar and the footer are already there, so you can focus on the show itself.

Take your time with each step. There's no rush, and every step saves automatically.

--- template
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <title>Friends · ShowShelf</title>
  <style>
    /* Two small rules so the page is easy to read in the Lab.
       Tomorrow you will write all the CSS yourself. */
    body { font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; max-width: 720px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 16px 32px; }
    img { max-width: 100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <header class="site-header">
    <a href="index.html" class="logo">ShowShelf</a>
    <nav>
      <a href="index.html">Home</a>
      <a href="about.html">About me</a>
    </nav>
  </header>

  <main>
    <article class="show">
{{slot:article}}
    </article>
  </main>

  <footer class="site-footer">
    <p>ShowShelf · Web Dev in 11 Days</p>
  </footer>
</body>
</html>


=== step heading
title: Your first HTML tag
minutes: 10
slot: article
--- explain
A web page is a plain text file written in **HTML**. HTML uses **tags** to say what each piece of text *is*: a heading, a paragraph, a picture.

A tag is a name inside angle brackets `< >`. Most tags come in pairs:

```html
<h1>The Simpsons</h1>
```

- `<h1>` is the **opening tag**.
- `</h1>` is the **closing tag**. It has a slash `/` before the name.
- The text between them is the **content**.

All three together are called an **element**. `h1` means *heading, level 1*: the main title of the page.

> The top bar ("ShowShelf") and the footer are already built. To see the whole file, click the **show.html (whole page)** tab above the dark box. Your code goes in the middle of that page.
--- task
In the dark code box, write a main heading that says **Friends**.

Watch the preview on the right while you type. Then press **Check**.
--- hint
A heading starts with `<h1>` and ends with `</h1>`. The show's name goes between them.
--- hint
Type exactly this: `<h1>Friends</h1>`
--- solution
<h1>Friends</h1>
--- check
const h1 = t.$("article h1");
if (!h1) {
  t.expect(!/friends/i.test(t.code), "I can see the word Friends, but it isn't inside an `<h1>` tag yet. Put `<h1>` before it and `</h1>` after it.");
  t.fail("I can't find an `<h1>` element yet. Start with `<h1>`, then the text, then `</h1>`.");
}
t.expect(/<\/h1>/i.test(t.code), "Your heading has no closing tag. Add `</h1>` after the text. Don't forget the slash.");
const txt = t.text(h1);
t.expect(txt.toLowerCase() === "friends", `Your heading says "${txt}". It should say **Friends**.`);
--- learned
You wrote your first **element**: opening tag, content, closing tag. The browser shows an `h1` big and bold because it is the most important heading on the page.
--- summary
An element is an opening tag, content and a closing tag. `<h1>` is the main heading.
--- mistakes
- Forgetting the slash in the closing tag: `<h1>Friends<h1>`.
- A space inside the brackets: `< h1>`.
- Writing in the preview instead of the dark code box.
--- teacher
The goal is only the idea of opening tag, content, closing tag. Let them try breaking it: delete the closing tag and see what happens (the browser guesses).


=== step paragraph
title: Add a paragraph
minutes: 10
slot: article
--- explain
Text that isn't a heading usually goes in a **paragraph**: the `<p>` element.

```html
<h1>The Simpsons</h1>
<p>A yellow family lives in the town of Springfield.</p>
```

One surprise: the browser ignores extra spaces and new lines in your code. These two are shown exactly the same:

```html
<p>A yellow family lives in the town of Springfield.</p>

<p>
  A yellow family lives
  in the town of Springfield.
</p>
```

The tags decide where things go, not the Enter key. That lets you split long text over several lines to keep your code easy to read.
--- task
Under your heading, add a paragraph with this text (copy it):

Six friends in their twenties and thirties share apartments, jobs, break-ups and a lot of coffee in New York City.
--- hint
Start a new line under your `</h1>`. Type `<p>`, then paste the text, then `</p>`.
--- hint
```html
<h1>Friends</h1>
<p>Six friends in their twenties ... in New York City.</p>
```
Use the whole sentence instead of the three dots.
--- solution
<h1>Friends</h1>

<p>
  Six friends in their twenties and thirties share apartments, jobs,
  break-ups and a lot of coffee in New York City.
</p>
--- check
t.expect(t.$("article h1"), "Your `<h1>` heading has gone. Keep it at the top, and add the paragraph under it.");
const p = t.$$("article p").find((x) => t.text(x).length > 20);
if (!p) {
  t.expect(!/six friends/i.test(t.code) || /<p/i.test(t.code), "The text is there, but it isn't inside a `<p>` element. Put `<p>` before it and `</p>` after it.");
  t.fail("I can't find a paragraph with the text yet. Add `<p>`, the text, and `</p>` under your heading.");
}
t.expect(/<\/p>/i.test(t.code), "Your paragraph needs a closing tag: `</p>`.");
--- learned
Paragraphs are **blocks**: each one starts on a new line with some space around it. And extra spaces or new lines in your code don't show on the page.
--- summary
`<p>` makes a paragraph. The browser ignores extra spaces and new lines in your code.
--- mistakes
- Putting the paragraph inside the heading: `<h1>Friends <p>...</p></h1>`.
- Forgetting `</p>` (the browser often guesses, but the check asks for it).


=== step emphasis
title: Bold and italic
minutes: 10
slot: article
--- explain
Elements can go **inside** other elements. This is called **nesting**.

- `<strong>` marks words as important. Browsers show them **bold**.
- `<em>` adds emphasis, like when you stress a word when speaking. Browsers show it *in italics*.

```html
<p>They live in <strong>Springfield</strong>, and Homer <em>loves</em> doughnuts.</p>
```

When you nest, close the inner element first, like boxes inside boxes:

```html
<p>This is <strong>right</strong>.</p>
<p>This is <strong>wrong.</p></strong>
```
--- task
In your paragraph:

1. make **New York City** bold with `<strong>`
2. make **a lot of coffee** italic with `<em>`
--- hint
Put `<strong>` just before "New York City" and `</strong>` just after it, still inside the paragraph.
--- hint
The end of your paragraph can look like this:
```html
break-ups and <em>a lot of coffee</em> in <strong>New York City</strong>.</p>
```
--- solution
<h1>Friends</h1>

<p>
  Six friends in their twenties and thirties share apartments, jobs,
  break-ups and <em>a lot of coffee</em> in <strong>New York City</strong>.
</p>
--- check
const strong = t.$("article p strong");
t.expect(strong, "I can't find a `<strong>` inside your paragraph yet. Wrap the words New York City: `<strong>New York City</strong>`.");
t.expect(/new york/i.test(t.text(strong)), `Your \`<strong>\` contains "${t.text(strong)}". Put it around **New York City**.`);
const em = t.$("article p em");
t.expect(em, "Now wrap **a lot of coffee** in `<em>` and `</em>`, inside the paragraph.");
t.expect(/coffee/i.test(t.text(em)), `Your \`<em>\` contains "${t.text(em)}". Put it around **a lot of coffee**.`);
t.expect(/<\/strong>/i.test(t.code) && /<\/em>/i.test(t.code), "Close each one: `</strong>` and `</em>`.");
--- learned
You **nested** elements: `strong` and `em` live inside the `p`. The rule for nesting: close the inner element before the outer one.
--- summary
Elements can be nested. `<strong>` is important (bold), `<em>` is emphasis (italic).
--- mistakes
- Closing in the wrong order: `<p>...<strong>...</p></strong>`.
- Using `<b>` and `<i>`. They also work, but `<strong>` and `<em>` say *why* the words are special.


=== step meta
title: Attributes and a class
minutes: 15
slot: article
--- explain
An opening tag can hold extra information called **attributes**. An attribute has a **name**, an equals sign and a **value** in quotes:

```html
<p class="meta">Fox · 1989 to today</p>
```

Here the attribute name is `class` and the value is `meta`.

A **class** is a label you choose. It doesn't change how the page looks yet, but tomorrow you'll use it in CSS to say "make every element with the label `meta` small and grey". Think of it as a sticky note on the element.
--- task
Between the heading and the paragraph, add a new paragraph with the class `meta` and this text:

NBC · 1994–2004 · about 22 minutes per episode

The dot `·` is hard to type on some keyboards. A normal dash `-` or a `|` is fine too.
--- hint
It's a normal `<p>` element with `class="meta"` inside the opening tag: `<p class="meta">`.
--- hint
```html
<h1>Friends</h1>
<p class="meta">NBC · 1994–2004 · about 22 minutes per episode</p>
```
--- solution
<h1>Friends</h1>
<p class="meta">NBC · 1994–2004 · about 22 minutes per episode</p>

<p>
  Six friends in their twenties and thirties share apartments, jobs,
  break-ups and <em>a lot of coffee</em> in <strong>New York City</strong>.
</p>
--- check
const meta = t.$("article .meta");
if (!meta) {
  t.expect(!/class\s*=\s*meta\b/i.test(t.code), "Put quotes around the class value: `class=\"meta\"`.");
  t.expect(!/clas+\s*=/i.test(t.code) || /class\s*=/i.test(t.code), "Check the spelling of the attribute: it's `class` (with two s).");
  t.fail("I can't find an element with `class=\"meta\"` yet. Add `<p class=\"meta\">...</p>` under the heading.");
}
t.expect(meta.tagName === "P", "Use a paragraph for the facts line: `<p class=\"meta\">`.");
t.expect(/nbc/i.test(t.text(meta)) && /1994/.test(t.text(meta)), "Your facts line should mention NBC and the years 1994–2004.");
t.expect(t.$("article strong") && t.$("article em"), "Keep your paragraph with `<strong>` and `<em>` from the last step.");
--- learned
You added an **attribute**: `class="meta"`. Attributes always go inside the opening tag, as `name="value"`. Tomorrow CSS will use the class to style this line.
--- summary
Attributes go in the opening tag as `name="value"`. A `class` is a label for CSS.
--- mistakes
- No quotes: `class=meta` (works in browsers, but always use quotes).
- The attribute in the closing tag: `</p class="meta">`.


=== step poster
title: Add the poster
minutes: 15
slot: article
--- explain
Pictures use the `<img>` element. It has **no closing tag** because it has no text content. Everything it needs is in its attributes:

```html
<img src="homer.png" alt="Homer Simpson eating a doughnut">
```

- `src` (source) is the file name or web address of the picture.
- `alt` (alternative text) describes the picture in words. Screen readers read it out for people who can't see the picture, and browsers show it when the picture can't load.

A file called **poster.svg** is ready for you in the Lab, next to your page.
--- task
Under the heading, add the poster:

- `src` is `poster.svg`
- `alt` describes it, for example "Poster for Friends"
- also add `class="poster"`, for tomorrow's CSS

Then try this: change `src` to a name that doesn't exist, like `posterr.svg`. What do you see? Change it back before you press Check.
--- hint
An `<img>` element with three attributes: `src`, `alt` and `class`. The order of attributes doesn't matter.
--- hint
```html
<img class="poster" src="poster.svg" alt="Poster for Friends">
```
--- solution
<h1>Friends</h1>
<p class="meta">NBC · 1994–2004 · about 22 minutes per episode</p>

<img class="poster" src="poster.svg" alt="Poster for Friends">

<p>
  Six friends in their twenties and thirties share apartments, jobs,
  break-ups and <em>a lot of coffee</em> in <strong>New York City</strong>.
</p>
--- check
const img = t.$("article img");
t.expect(img, "I can't find an `<img>` yet. Add `<img src=\"poster.svg\" alt=\"Poster for Friends\">` under the heading.");
t.expect(/poster\.svg/.test(t.code), "The `src` should be exactly `poster.svg`. Check the spelling.");
t.expect(img.complete && img.naturalWidth > 0, "The picture didn't load. Check that `src=\"poster.svg\"` is spelled exactly like that, with quotes.");
t.expect((img.getAttribute("alt") || "").trim().length > 2, "Your image needs `alt` text that describes it, for example `alt=\"Poster for Friends\"`.");
t.expect(img.classList.contains("poster"), "Add `class=\"poster\"` to the image too. We'll need it tomorrow.");
t.expect(!/<\/img>/i.test(t.code), "`<img>` has no closing tag. Remove the `</img>`.");
--- learned
`<img>` is an **empty element**: no content and no closing tag. `src` says where the picture is, and `alt` says what it shows. When the file is missing, the browser shows the alt text instead.
--- summary
`<img src="..." alt="...">` shows a picture. It has no closing tag, and `alt` describes the picture.
--- mistakes
- Writing `<img src="poster.svg"></img>`.
- Leaving out `alt`, or writing "image" as the alt text (describe what it shows instead).
- Typing `scr` instead of `src`.


=== step genres
title: Subheadings and a list
minutes: 15
slot: article
--- explain
Headings come in six levels, `<h1>` to `<h6>`. The page has one `<h1>` for its title. Parts of the page get `<h2>`, and smaller parts inside them get `<h3>`.

A **list** of items uses two elements together:

- `<ul>` (*unordered list*) is the whole list. It shows bullets.
- `<li>` (*list item*) is one item. Every `<li>` goes inside the `<ul>`.

```html
<h2>Characters</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Homer</li>
  <li>Marge</li>
</ul>
```

Notice the spaces before each `<li>`. They don't change the page, but they show that the items are inside the list. This is called **indenting**.
--- task
Under your paragraph, add:

1. a subheading `<h2>` that says **Genres**
2. a bulleted list with two items: **Comedy** and **Romance**
--- hint
First the `<h2>Genres</h2>`, then `<ul>` on its own line, then one `<li>` per genre, then `</ul>`.
--- hint
```html
<h2>Genres</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Comedy</li>
  <li>Romance</li>
</ul>
```
--- solution
<h1>Friends</h1>
<p class="meta">NBC · 1994–2004 · about 22 minutes per episode</p>

<img class="poster" src="poster.svg" alt="Poster for Friends">

<p>
  Six friends in their twenties and thirties share apartments, jobs,
  break-ups and <em>a lot of coffee</em> in <strong>New York City</strong>.
</p>

<h2>Genres</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Comedy</li>
  <li>Romance</li>
</ul>
--- check
const h2 = t.$$("article h2").find((h) => /genre/i.test(t.text(h)));
t.expect(h2, "I can't find an `<h2>` that says Genres yet.");
const lis = t.$$("article li");
t.expect(lis.length >= 2, "Add a list with two `<li>` items: Comedy and Romance.");
t.expect(lis.every((li) => li.parentElement.tagName === "UL" || li.parentElement.tagName === "OL"), "Every `<li>` must be inside a `<ul>`. Put `<ul>` before the items and `</ul>` after them.");
const ul = t.$$("article ul").find((u) => /comedy/i.test(t.text(u)));
t.expect(ul, "Your list should have an item that says Comedy.");
t.expect(/romance/i.test(t.text(ul)), "Add a second item that says Romance.");
t.expect(ul.children.length >= 2 && [...ul.children].every((c) => c.tagName === "LI"), "Inside the `<ul>` put only `<li>` items, one per genre.");
--- learned
A list is two kinds of elements working together: one `<ul>` around several `<li>`. And `<h2>` headings split the page into parts, like chapters in a book.
--- summary
`<h2>` for parts of the page. `<ul>` with `<li>` items makes a bulleted list.
--- mistakes
- `<li>` items without a `<ul>` around them.
- Two separate lists, one per item, instead of one list with two items.


=== step cast
title: A longer list: the cast
minutes: 10
slot: article
--- explain
You'll write lists all the time, so let's practise once more. Lists can be as long as you like. Every item gets its own `<li>`.

Good habits that make long lists easy to read:

- one `<li>` per line
- indent the items with two spaces
- the closing `</ul>` lines up with the opening `<ul>`

> Try the **Tab** key in the code box: it adds two spaces.
--- task
Under the genres list, add an `<h2>` that says **Main cast** and a list with **at least three** of these actors:

- Jennifer Aniston as Rachel Green
- Courteney Cox as Monica Geller
- Lisa Kudrow as Phoebe Buffay
- Matt LeBlanc as Joey Tribbiani
- Matthew Perry as Chandler Bing
- David Schwimmer as Ross Geller
--- hint
It's the same pattern as the genres: `<h2>`, then `<ul>`, then one `<li>` per actor, then `</ul>`.
--- hint
```html
<h2>Main cast</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Jennifer Aniston as Rachel Green</li>
  <li>Courteney Cox as Monica Geller</li>
  <li>Lisa Kudrow as Phoebe Buffay</li>
</ul>
```
--- solution
<h1>Friends</h1>
<p class="meta">NBC · 1994–2004 · about 22 minutes per episode</p>

<img class="poster" src="poster.svg" alt="Poster for Friends">

<p>
  Six friends in their twenties and thirties share apartments, jobs,
  break-ups and <em>a lot of coffee</em> in <strong>New York City</strong>.
</p>

<h2>Genres</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Comedy</li>
  <li>Romance</li>
</ul>

<h2>Main cast</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Jennifer Aniston as Rachel Green</li>
  <li>Courteney Cox as Monica Geller</li>
  <li>Lisa Kudrow as Phoebe Buffay</li>
  <li>Matt LeBlanc as Joey Tribbiani</li>
  <li>Matthew Perry as Chandler Bing</li>
  <li>David Schwimmer as Ross Geller</li>
</ul>
--- check
t.expect(t.$$("article h2").some((h) => /cast/i.test(t.text(h))), "Add an `<h2>` that says Main cast.");
const lists = t.$$("article ul");
t.expect(lists.length >= 2, "Add a second `<ul>` list for the cast, under the heading Main cast.");
const cast = lists.find((u) => u.querySelectorAll("li").length >= 3 && !/comedy/i.test(t.text(u)));
t.expect(cast, "Your cast list needs at least three `<li>` items, one actor each.");
t.expect(t.$$("article li").every((li) => /^(UL|OL)$/.test(li.parentElement.tagName)), "Every `<li>` must be inside a list (`<ul>`).");
--- learned
Same pattern, longer list. Indenting the `<li>` lines makes it easy to see what's inside what, which matters more as pages grow.
--- summary
Long lists: one `<li>` per line, indented inside the `<ul>`.
--- mistakes
- Putting all actors in one `<li>` separated by commas.
- Forgetting the new `<h2>`.


=== step top3
title: A numbered list
minutes: 10
slot: article
--- explain
When the **order** matters, use `<ol>` (*ordered list*) instead of `<ul>`. The browser numbers the items for you:

```html
<h2>How to make toast</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Put bread in the toaster</li>
  <li>Wait</li>
  <li>Eat</li>
</ol>
```

You don't type the numbers. If you move an item up, the numbers update by themselves.
--- task
Add an `<h2>` that says **My favourite characters** and an ordered list of your top three. Your opinion, your order.
--- hint
Same as before, but `<ol>` and `</ol>` instead of `<ul>` and `</ul>`.
--- hint
```html
<h2>My favourite characters</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Phoebe</li>
  <li>Chandler</li>
  <li>Joey</li>
</ol>
```
--- solution
<h1>Friends</h1>
<p class="meta">NBC · 1994–2004 · about 22 minutes per episode</p>

<img class="poster" src="poster.svg" alt="Poster for Friends">

<p>
  Six friends in their twenties and thirties share apartments, jobs,
  break-ups and <em>a lot of coffee</em> in <strong>New York City</strong>.
</p>

<h2>Genres</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Comedy</li>
  <li>Romance</li>
</ul>

<h2>Main cast</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Jennifer Aniston as Rachel Green</li>
  <li>Courteney Cox as Monica Geller</li>
  <li>Lisa Kudrow as Phoebe Buffay</li>
  <li>Matt LeBlanc as Joey Tribbiani</li>
  <li>Matthew Perry as Chandler Bing</li>
  <li>David Schwimmer as Ross Geller</li>
</ul>

<h2>My favourite characters</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Phoebe</li>
  <li>Chandler</li>
  <li>Joey</li>
</ol>
--- check
const ol = t.$("article ol");
t.expect(ol, "I can't find an `<ol>` yet. It works like `<ul>`, but with numbers.");
t.expect(ol.querySelectorAll("li").length >= 3, "Your numbered list needs three `<li>` items.");
t.expect(!/^\s*\d/.test(t.text(ol.querySelector("li"))), "No need to type the numbers yourself: `<ol>` adds them. Remove the numbers from your items.");
t.expect(t.$$("article h2").length >= 3, "Add an `<h2>` heading above the numbered list, like \"My favourite characters\".");
--- learned
`<ol>` numbers the items for you. Choosing `<ul>` or `<ol>` is about meaning: does the order matter?
--- summary
`<ol>` is a numbered list, for when the order matters.
--- mistakes
- Typing "1." inside each item (you get numbers twice).


=== step link
title: A link to another website
minutes: 15
slot: article
--- explain
Links are what make the web a *web*. A link uses the `<a>` element (*anchor*):

```html
<a href="https://www.wikipedia.org">Read more on Wikipedia</a>
```

- `href` is the address the link goes to.
- The content ("Read more on Wikipedia") is the text you click.

A full web address starts with `https://`. Without it, the browser looks for a file with that name on *your* website.

Links are **inline**: they sit inside text, so we put this one inside a paragraph.
--- task
At the bottom, add a paragraph with a link to the show's page on TVmaze, a free website about TV shows:

`https://www.tvmaze.com/shows/431/friends`

Use any link text you like, for example "Read more on TVmaze". Click the link in the preview when you're done: it opens in a new tab.
--- hint
A `<p>` with an `<a>` inside it. The address goes in `href="..."`, and the text you click goes between `<a ...>` and `</a>`.
--- hint
```html
<p>
  Want more? <a href="https://www.tvmaze.com/shows/431/friends">Read about it on TVmaze</a>.
</p>
```
--- solution
<h1>Friends</h1>
<p class="meta">NBC · 1994–2004 · about 22 minutes per episode</p>

<img class="poster" src="poster.svg" alt="Poster for Friends">

<p>
  Six friends in their twenties and thirties share apartments, jobs,
  break-ups and <em>a lot of coffee</em> in <strong>New York City</strong>.
</p>

<h2>Genres</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Comedy</li>
  <li>Romance</li>
</ul>

<h2>Main cast</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Jennifer Aniston as Rachel Green</li>
  <li>Courteney Cox as Monica Geller</li>
  <li>Lisa Kudrow as Phoebe Buffay</li>
  <li>Matt LeBlanc as Joey Tribbiani</li>
  <li>Matthew Perry as Chandler Bing</li>
  <li>David Schwimmer as Ross Geller</li>
</ul>

<h2>My favourite characters</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Phoebe</li>
  <li>Chandler</li>
  <li>Joey</li>
</ol>

<p>
  Want more? <a href="https://www.tvmaze.com/shows/431/friends">Read about it on TVmaze</a>.
</p>
--- check
const a = t.$("article a");
t.expect(a, "I can't find a link yet. Add `<a href=\"...\">link text</a>` inside a paragraph.");
const href = a.getAttribute("href") || "";
t.expect(href, "Your link needs an `href` attribute with the address: `href=\"https://www.tvmaze.com/shows/431/friends\"`.");
t.expect(/tvmaze\.com/i.test(href), `Your link goes to "${href}". Point it at the TVmaze page for Friends.`);
t.expect(/^https?:\/\//i.test(href), "The address must start with `https://`. Without it, the browser looks for a file on your own site.");
t.expect(t.text(a).length > 1, "Your link has no text to click. Put some text between `<a ...>` and `</a>`.");
t.expect(a.parentElement.tagName === "P", "Put the link inside a paragraph: `<p>` ... `<a ...>...</a>` ... `</p>`.");
--- learned
`<a href="...">` makes a link. The `href` is where it goes; the content is what people click. Full addresses start with `https://`.
--- summary
`<a href="https://...">text</a>` is a link to another page.
--- mistakes
- Leaving out `https://`, so the link points to a missing file.
- Putting the address as the link text but forgetting `href`.


=== step table
title: A table of seasons
minutes: 20
slot: article
--- explain
Some information is best shown in rows and columns, like a spreadsheet. HTML has a **table** for that:

- `<table>` is the whole table.
- `<tr>` (*table row*) is one row.
- `<th>` (*table heading*) is a heading cell. It's bold.
- `<td>` (*table data*) is a normal cell.

Every row has the same number of cells, in the same order as the headings.

We've added the start of a seasons table to your code: the headings and season 1.
--- task
Add two more rows to the table:

- season **2**: **24** episodes, **1995**
- season **3**: **25** episodes, **1996**

Each new row is a `<tr>` with three `<td>` cells, just like the season 1 row.
--- starter-append
<h2>Seasons</h2>
<table>
  <tr>
    <th>Season</th>
    <th>Episodes</th>
    <th>Year</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>1</td>
    <td>24</td>
    <td>1994</td>
  </tr>
</table>
--- hint
Copy the whole season 1 row, from `<tr>` to `</tr>`, paste it before `</table>`, and change the three numbers.
--- hint
```html
  <tr>
    <td>2</td>
    <td>24</td>
    <td>1995</td>
  </tr>
```
Then one more row like it for season 3.
--- solution
<h1>Friends</h1>
<p class="meta">NBC · 1994–2004 · about 22 minutes per episode</p>

<img class="poster" src="poster.svg" alt="Poster for Friends">

<p>
  Six friends in their twenties and thirties share apartments, jobs,
  break-ups and <em>a lot of coffee</em> in <strong>New York City</strong>.
</p>

<h2>Genres</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Comedy</li>
  <li>Romance</li>
</ul>

<h2>Main cast</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Jennifer Aniston as Rachel Green</li>
  <li>Courteney Cox as Monica Geller</li>
  <li>Lisa Kudrow as Phoebe Buffay</li>
  <li>Matt LeBlanc as Joey Tribbiani</li>
  <li>Matthew Perry as Chandler Bing</li>
  <li>David Schwimmer as Ross Geller</li>
</ul>

<h2>My favourite characters</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Phoebe</li>
  <li>Chandler</li>
  <li>Joey</li>
</ol>

<h2>Seasons</h2>
<table>
  <tr>
    <th>Season</th>
    <th>Episodes</th>
    <th>Year</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>1</td>
    <td>24</td>
    <td>1994</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>2</td>
    <td>24</td>
    <td>1995</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>3</td>
    <td>25</td>
    <td>1996</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<p>
  Want more? <a href="https://www.tvmaze.com/shows/431/friends">Read about it on TVmaze</a>.
</p>
--- check
const table = t.$("article table");
t.expect(table, "The table has gone. Press **Start again** to get it back.");
const rows = [...table.querySelectorAll("tr")];
const data = rows.filter((r) => r.querySelector("td"));
t.expect(data.length >= 3, `Your table has ${data.length} season row${data.length === 1 ? "" : "s"}. Add rows for seasons 2 and 3, each a \`<tr>\` with three \`<td>\`.`);
t.expect(data.every((r) => r.querySelectorAll("td").length === 3), "Each season row needs exactly three `<td>` cells: season, episodes, year.");
const cells = data.map((r) => [...r.querySelectorAll("td")].map((c) => t.text(c)));
t.expect(cells.some((c) => c[0] === "2" && c[2] === "1995"), "I can't find the row for season 2 (24 episodes, 1995).");
t.expect(cells.some((c) => c[0] === "3" && c[1] === "25" && c[2] === "1996"), "I can't find the row for season 3 (25 episodes, 1996).");
t.expect((t.code.match(/<tr/gi) || []).length === (t.code.match(/<\/tr>/gi) || []).length, "Each `<tr>` needs its own closing `</tr>`. Count them: the numbers should match.");
--- learned
A table is rows (`<tr>`) of cells (`<th>` for headings, `<td>` for data). The copy-paste-change trick you just used is how most developers write repeated HTML.
--- summary
Tables: `<table>`, rows `<tr>`, heading cells `<th>`, data cells `<td>`.
--- mistakes
- Pasting the new row after `</table>` instead of before it.
- A row with only two `<td>`: the columns shift.
- Missing `</tr>` between rows.


=== step sections
title: Group it with sections
minutes: 15
slot: article
--- explain
Look at the **show.html (whole page)** tab. The page is split into big parts with elements that say what each part is:

- `<header>`: the top of the page (logo and menu)
- `<main>`: the main content
- `<article>`: one complete thing, here one show
- `<footer>`: the bottom of the page

These are called **semantic** elements: their names describe their meaning. They don't change how the page looks, but they help screen readers, search engines and other developers understand your page.

Inside the article, `<section>` groups a heading with the content that belongs to it:

```html
<section>
  <h2>Characters</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Homer</li>
  </ul>
</section>
```
--- task
Wrap **Genres** (the `<h2>` and its list) in a `<section>`. Then do the same for **Main cast**.

If you like, wrap the other two parts as well. Indent the lines inside each section by two spaces.
--- hint
Type `<section>` on a new line just above `<h2>Genres</h2>`, and `</section>` on a new line just below the `</ul>` of that list.
--- hint
```html
<section>
  <h2>Genres</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Comedy</li>
    <li>Romance</li>
  </ul>
</section>
```
To indent many lines at once, select them and press Tab.
--- solution
<h1>Friends</h1>
<p class="meta">NBC · 1994–2004 · about 22 minutes per episode</p>

<img class="poster" src="poster.svg" alt="Poster for Friends">

<p>
  Six friends in their twenties and thirties share apartments, jobs,
  break-ups and <em>a lot of coffee</em> in <strong>New York City</strong>.
</p>

<section>
  <h2>Genres</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Comedy</li>
    <li>Romance</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Main cast</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Jennifer Aniston as Rachel Green</li>
    <li>Courteney Cox as Monica Geller</li>
    <li>Lisa Kudrow as Phoebe Buffay</li>
    <li>Matt LeBlanc as Joey Tribbiani</li>
    <li>Matthew Perry as Chandler Bing</li>
    <li>David Schwimmer as Ross Geller</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>My favourite characters</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Phoebe</li>
    <li>Chandler</li>
    <li>Joey</li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Seasons</h2>
  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Season</th>
      <th>Episodes</th>
      <th>Year</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>1</td>
      <td>24</td>
      <td>1994</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>2</td>
      <td>24</td>
      <td>1995</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>3</td>
      <td>25</td>
      <td>1996</td>
    </tr>
  </table>
</section>

<p>
  Want more? <a href="https://www.tvmaze.com/shows/431/friends">Read about it on TVmaze</a>.
</p>
--- check
const secs = t.$$("article section");
t.expect(secs.length >= 2, secs.length ? "Good start. Now wrap the Main cast part in a `<section>` too." : "I can't find a `<section>` yet. Put `<section>` above `<h2>Genres</h2>` and `</section>` after its list.");
t.expect(secs.every((s) => s.querySelector("h2")), "Each section should start with its `<h2>` heading, inside the section.");
const genre = secs.find((s) => /genre/i.test(t.text(s.querySelector("h2"))));
t.expect(genre && genre.querySelector("ul"), "The Genres section should hold both the heading and its list: `</section>` goes after `</ul>`.");
const cast = secs.find((s) => /cast/i.test(t.text(s.querySelector("h2"))));
t.expect(cast && cast.querySelectorAll("li").length >= 3, "Wrap Main cast (heading and list) in a `<section>` as well.");
t.expect(!secs.some((s) => s.querySelectorAll("h2").length > 1), "Each section should hold one part only. Check where each `</section>` is.");
t.expect((t.code.match(/<section/gi) || []).length === (t.code.match(/<\/section>/gi) || []).length, "Each `<section>` needs its own `</section>`. Count them: the numbers should match.");
--- learned
Semantic elements (`header`, `main`, `article`, `section`, `footer`) describe the **structure** of the page. Nothing changed on screen, but the page is now much easier to understand for machines and for people reading your code.
--- summary
Semantic elements (`header`, `main`, `article`, `section`, `footer`) describe the parts of a page.
--- mistakes
- One big section around everything.
- `</section>` placed between the heading and its list.


=== step comments
title: Notes for humans
minutes: 10
slot: article
--- explain
Sometimes you want to leave a note in your code for yourself or another developer. HTML **comments** are ignored by the browser:

```html
<!-- The seasons table: one row per season -->
```

A comment starts with `<!--` and ends with `-->`. It can be one line or many.

Comments are also handy to hide something temporarily without deleting it: put `<!--` before it and `-->` after it.
--- task
1. Add a comment above the seasons section that says what it is.
2. Try hiding the "My favourite characters" section with a comment, look at the preview, then remove the comment marks again so it comes back.
--- hint
A comment looks like this: `<!-- your note here -->`. Put it on its own line, above `<section>`.
--- hint
```html
<!-- Seasons: one row per season -->
<section>
  <h2>Seasons</h2>
```
--- solution
<h1>Friends</h1>
<p class="meta">NBC · 1994–2004 · about 22 minutes per episode</p>

<img class="poster" src="poster.svg" alt="Poster for Friends">

<p>
  Six friends in their twenties and thirties share apartments, jobs,
  break-ups and <em>a lot of coffee</em> in <strong>New York City</strong>.
</p>

<section>
  <h2>Genres</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Comedy</li>
    <li>Romance</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Main cast</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Jennifer Aniston as Rachel Green</li>
    <li>Courteney Cox as Monica Geller</li>
    <li>Lisa Kudrow as Phoebe Buffay</li>
    <li>Matt LeBlanc as Joey Tribbiani</li>
    <li>Matthew Perry as Chandler Bing</li>
    <li>David Schwimmer as Ross Geller</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>My favourite characters</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Phoebe</li>
    <li>Chandler</li>
    <li>Joey</li>
  </ol>
</section>

<!-- Seasons: one row per season -->
<section>
  <h2>Seasons</h2>
  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Season</th>
      <th>Episodes</th>
      <th>Year</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>1</td>
      <td>24</td>
      <td>1994</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>2</td>
      <td>24</td>
      <td>1995</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>3</td>
      <td>25</td>
      <td>1996</td>
    </tr>
  </table>
</section>

<p>
  Want more? <a href="https://www.tvmaze.com/shows/431/friends">Read about it on TVmaze</a>.
</p>
--- check
const comments = t.code.match(/<!--([\s\S]*?)-->/g) || [];
t.expect(comments.length, "I can't find a comment yet. Write `<!-- a note -->` above the seasons section.");
t.expect(comments.some((c) => c.replace(/<!--|-->/g, "").trim().length > 2), "Write a few words inside your comment, between `<!--` and `-->`.");
t.expect(t.$("article table"), "The seasons table is hidden. Make sure your comment is closed with `-->` straight after your note.");
t.expect(t.$$("article ol li").length >= 3, "Your favourite characters are still hidden. Remove the `<!--` and `-->` around that section.");
--- learned
Comments `<!-- -->` are notes for humans. The browser skips them, which also makes them a quick way to switch a piece of HTML off and on again.
--- summary
`<!-- comments -->` are notes the browser ignores.
--- mistakes
- A comment that never closes (no `-->`): everything after it disappears.


=== step spoiler
title: A spoiler you have to click
minutes: 10
slot: article
bonus: yes
--- explain
HTML has an element that can open and close without any programming: `<details>`. The `<summary>` inside it is the part you always see and click; the rest is hidden until then.

```html
<details>
  <summary>Who shot Mr Burns?</summary>
  <p>Maggie!</p>
</details>
```
--- task
Add a `<details>` element with a `<summary>` like "Spoiler: how does the show end?" and a paragraph inside with your answer (or "Watch it and find out!").

Click it in the preview to open and close it.
--- hint
The `<summary>` must be the first thing inside `<details>`. The paragraph goes after it, still inside `<details>`.
--- hint
```html
<details>
  <summary>Spoiler: how does the show end?</summary>
  <p>Watch it and find out!</p>
</details>
```
--- solution-append
<details>
  <summary>Spoiler: how does the show end?</summary>
  <p>Watch it and find out!</p>
</details>
--- check
const d = t.$("article details");
t.expect(d, "I can't find a `<details>` element yet.");
const s = d.querySelector("summary");
t.expect(s && t.text(s).length > 2, "Put a `<summary>` with some text inside your `<details>`.");
t.expect(d.querySelector("p"), "Add a paragraph inside `<details>`, after the summary. That's the hidden part.");
--- learned
`<details>` and `<summary>` make a click-to-open box, with no JavaScript at all. HTML can do more than you'd think.
--- summary
`<details>` with `<summary>` makes a box that opens on click.


=== step figure
title: A poster with a caption
minutes: 10
slot: article
bonus: yes
--- explain
A picture with a caption belongs in a `<figure>`. The caption goes in `<figcaption>`:

```html
<figure>
  <img src="homer.png" alt="Homer Simpson">
  <figcaption>Homer in season 1</figcaption>
</figure>
```
--- task
Wrap your poster `<img>` in a `<figure>` and add a `<figcaption>` under it, for example "The official poster".
--- hint
`<figure>` goes on the line before the `<img>`; the `<figcaption>` and `</figure>` go after it.
--- hint
```html
<figure>
  <img class="poster" src="poster.svg" alt="Poster for Friends">
  <figcaption>The official poster</figcaption>
</figure>
```
--- solution-replace
<img class="poster" src="poster.svg" alt="Poster for Friends">
=>
<figure>
  <img class="poster" src="poster.svg" alt="Poster for Friends">
  <figcaption>The official poster</figcaption>
</figure>
--- check
const f = t.$("article figure");
t.expect(f, "I can't find a `<figure>` yet.");
t.expect(f.querySelector("img"), "Put the poster `<img>` inside the `<figure>`.");
const c = f.querySelector("figcaption");
t.expect(c && t.text(c).length > 2, "Add a `<figcaption>` with some text inside the `<figure>`.");
--- learned
`<figure>` and `<figcaption>` connect a picture with its caption, another semantic pair.
--- summary
`<figure>` + `<figcaption>`: a picture with a caption.


=== step another
title: Add a second show
minutes: 20
slot: article
bonus: yes
--- explain
Now use everything from today on your own. Pick a show you like and add a short part about it at the bottom of the page.

A horizontal line `<hr>` is a good way to separate it. Like `<img>`, `<hr>` has no closing tag.
--- task
At the bottom, add:

1. an `<hr>`
2. an `<h2>` with the name of a show you like
3. a paragraph about it
4. a list of at least two things (genres, characters, anything)
5. optional: a link to its page on TVmaze (search for it on tvmaze.com)
--- hint
Use the Friends part of your page as a pattern: heading, paragraph, list.
--- hint
```html
<hr>
<h2>The Office</h2>
<p>A camera crew follows the everyday chaos of a small paper company office.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Comedy</li>
  <li>Michael Scott is the boss</li>
</ul>
```
--- solution-append
<hr>
<h2>The Office</h2>
<p>A camera crew follows the everyday chaos of a small paper company office.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Comedy</li>
  <li>Michael Scott is the boss</li>
</ul>
--- check
const hr = t.$("article hr");
t.expect(hr, "Start with an `<hr>` line to separate the new show.");
let el = hr.nextElementSibling, h2 = null, p = null, list = null;
while (el) {
  if (el.tagName === "H2" && !h2) h2 = el;
  if (el.tagName === "P" && !p) p = el;
  if (/^(UL|OL)$/.test(el.tagName) && !list) list = el;
  if (el.tagName === "SECTION") { h2 = h2 || el.querySelector("h2"); p = p || el.querySelector("p"); list = list || el.querySelector("ul,ol"); }
  el = el.nextElementSibling;
}
t.expect(h2, "After the `<hr>`, add an `<h2>` with the name of a show.");
t.expect(p && t.text(p).length > 10, "Add a paragraph that says something about the show.");
t.expect(list && list.querySelectorAll("li").length >= 2, "Add a list with at least two items about the show.");
--- learned
You built a part of a page on your own, from memory. That's exactly what the first day is for.
--- summary
You can build a page section from scratch: heading, text, list, link.
